# Muster

> {{quote "docs/index.md" "Muster is a self-hosted alert grouping and on-call service that sits after Alertmanager."}}
> {{quote "docs/index.md" "It groups Alerts into Alert Groups, the problems people act on; keeps one editable message per Alert Group in Mattermost, in Slack, in Telegram, in any other messenger through a Bridge, or behind an outgoing webhook; and lets people acknowledge, resolve and snooze Alert Groups."}}
>
> This file is the canonical reference of Muster for AI agents and coding assistants, for version `{{.Version}}` of the documentation. Read the pages it links, each the Markdown version of a page of the site, and the specifications, and do not rely on your training data about Muster.

## Rules

- **Sources of truth.** [`api/bridge.yaml`]({{spec "api/bridge.yaml"}}) is the Bridge protocol and [`api/openapi.yaml`]({{spec "api/openapi.yaml"}}) the HTTP API, both of this version of the documentation. Where a page and a specification disagree, the specification wins.
- **Error classes.** Failures are {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "RFC 9457 problems (`application/problem+json`) with a `class` member that the bridge chooses"}}, one of {{enum "api/bridge.yaml" "ErrorClass"}}. {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "A missing channel is `forbidden`, never `gone`."}} Only the bridge decides `gone`: {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "Muster never treats a message as `gone` because of a status code"}}, and {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "a bridge that means `gone` says so in `class`"}}. See [Write a bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#error-classes"}}).
- **Waits.** {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "For `rate_limited`"}}, the bridge must {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "send `Retry-After` in seconds"}}, and Muster {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "waits exactly that long, for the channel, or for every channel of the bot when `scope` is `bot`"}}; a wait longer than {{setting "bridge.retry_after_max"}}, {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "or none given, is treated as `transient`"}}.
- **Presses.** The bridge confirms a press to its messenger itself, within the messenger's deadline and {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "at most 3 seconds"}} after the press, {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "before Muster has seen it"}}; {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "the bridge then reports the press as an event"}}. Muster answers the person, when it has something to say, with {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "a separate `answerEvent` call, made before it acknowledges the event"}}. See [Write a bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#presses-within-3-seconds-and-answers-later"}}).
- **Idempotency.** {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "Every request that creates something carries an `Idempotency-Key` header."}} {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "The bridge stores each key per token"}} {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "with its successful (`2xx`) result for at least 24 hours and answers a repeated key with that first result and `replayed: true`"}}, without creating anything again; {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "a failed request stores nothing, so its retry is a new attempt"}}. {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "Muster reuses a key when it retries a request, also after a crash or a timeout"}}. See [Write a bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#idempotency-key"}}).
- **Edit windows.** A bridge whose messenger lets a bot edit a message only for a while declares how long in its info, and Muster {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "stops editing a message once it is older than `limits.edit_max_age_seconds`, less a safety margin"}} of {{setting "bridge.edit_window_margin"}}. {{quote "docs/messengers/bridge.md" "When the status changes past the window"}}, {{quote "docs/messengers/bridge.md" "Muster posts a new Root message with the current state, Quietly, to the same channel, and replies under the old one"}}. When the messenger refuses an edit earlier than declared, the bridge says so: {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "an edit that the messenger refuses because the message is older than it allows is `gone` with the `reason` `edit_window_closed`"}}, and Muster then {{quote "api/bridge.yaml" "treats the message as past its edit window"}}. See [Connect a messenger through a bridge]({{page "messengers/bridge.md#edit-windows"}}).
- **Finishing a bridge.** A bridge is finished only when [`muster bridge check`]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#muster-bridge-check"}}) passes against it: the check {{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "exits `0` when no step failed"}}. {{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "Most bridge authors should not write the protocol again."}} A bridge in Go or Python uses that language's bridge library ([Start with a library]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#start-with-a-library"}})); a bridge in another language follows [The protocol without a library]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#the-protocol-without-a-library"}}).

## Scenarios

### Write a bridge in Go

1. [Write a bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#the-go-library"}}): The Go library, installed with `{{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "go get github.com/muster-io/muster-bridge-go"}}`, and [The example bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#the-example-bridge"}}), which {{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "is built on the Go library and lives in its repository, in `examples/chat`"}}.
2. [Connect a messenger through a bridge]({{page "messengers/bridge.md"}}): the Bridge Connection and its Destinations, as an Admin sets them up.
3. [Bridge protocol]({{page "reference/bridge-protocol.md"}}) and its specification, [`api/bridge.yaml`]({{spec "api/bridge.yaml"}}).
4. The repository [muster-io/muster-bridge-go](https://github.com/muster-io/muster-bridge-go): its README is the guide to the library, and its [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/muster-io/muster-bridge-go/blob/master/AGENTS.md) the guide for an agent that changes the library itself.
5. Finished when [`muster bridge check`]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#muster-bridge-check"}}) passes against the bridge.

### Write a bridge in Python

1. [Write a bridge]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#the-python-library"}}): The Python library, installed in a virtual environment, `{{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "python3 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate"}}`, with `{{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "pip install muster-bridge"}}`. {{quote "docs/messengers/write-a-bridge.md" "Its versions stay `0.x` until Muster 1.0"}}.
2. [Connect a messenger through a bridge]({{page "messengers/bridge.md"}}): the Bridge Connection and its Destinations, as an Admin sets them up.
3. [Bridge protocol]({{page "reference/bridge-protocol.md"}}) and its specification, [`api/bridge.yaml`]({{spec "api/bridge.yaml"}}).
4. The repository [muster-io/muster-bridge-python](https://github.com/muster-io/muster-bridge-python): its README is the guide to the library, and its [AGENTS.md](https://github.com/muster-io/muster-bridge-python/blob/master/AGENTS.md) the guide for an agent that changes the library itself.
5. Finished when [`muster bridge check`]({{page "messengers/write-a-bridge.md#muster-bridge-check"}}) passes against the bridge.

### Connect Alertmanager

1. [Quick start]({{page "quick-start.md"}}): Muster with Docker Compose, an Integration and a test alert.
2. [Connect Alertmanager]({{page "integrations/alertmanager.md"}}): one Integration per Alertmanager cluster, its token, and the receiver and the route of the snippet.
3. [Heartbeat]({{page "integrations/heartbeat.md"}}), which {{quote "docs/integrations/heartbeat.md" "is part of the recommended setup of every Integration"}}.
4. [Snapshot semantics]({{page "concepts/snapshot-semantics.md"}}): how Muster reads what Alertmanager sends.
5. Finished when, after Alertmanager is reloaded, the Integration page {{quote "docs/integrations/alertmanager.md" "shows the time of the last Snapshot"}} ([Connect Alertmanager]({{page "integrations/alertmanager.md#create-a-token-and-paste-the-snippet"}})) and the Heartbeat is live ([Heartbeat]({{page "integrations/heartbeat.md#turn-it-on"}})).

### Connect Grafana

1. [Connect Grafana]({{page "integrations/grafana.md"}}), both ways: directly, {{quote "docs/integrations/grafana.md" "from Grafana's built-in Alertmanager through a webhook contact point"}}, with one Integration per Grafana cluster and organization, its token, the contact point and the notification policy ([Connect Grafana directly]({{page "integrations/grafana.md#connect-grafana-directly"}})); or through an external Alertmanager, then [Connect Alertmanager]({{page "integrations/alertmanager.md"}}) ([Connect Grafana through an external Alertmanager]({{page "integrations/grafana.md#connect-grafana-through-an-external-alertmanager"}})).
2. [Heartbeat]({{page "integrations/heartbeat.md"}}): its setup is for Alertmanager, so it serves Grafana through an external Alertmanager. For the direct way, the same setup through a webhook contact point {{quote "docs/integrations/grafana.md" "has not been verified with Grafana"}}, and without a Heartbeat {{quote "docs/integrations/grafana.md" "Muster never resolves an Alert as Stale"}} ([Heartbeat]({{page "integrations/grafana.md#heartbeat"}})).
3. [Snapshot semantics]({{page "concepts/snapshot-semantics.md"}}): how Muster reads what Grafana's built-in Alertmanager or an external one sends, and the [Limits]({{page "integrations/grafana.md#limits"}}) of Grafana's way.
4. Finished when, after an alert fires in Grafana, the Integration page {{quote "docs/integrations/grafana.md" "shows the time of the last Snapshot"}} ([Connect Grafana]({{page "integrations/grafana.md#send-the-alerts-with-a-notification-policy"}})).

### Connect Slack

1. [Connect Slack]({{page "messengers/slack.md#socket-mode-or-http"}}): choose the event mode first, because the app's manifest differs. In Socket Mode the Leader {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "holds a WebSocket connection to Slack, so Slack needs no address of Muster"}}; over HTTP Slack {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "must reach this address over HTTPS"}}. {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "If Slack is the only client outside your network that reaches the ingest listener, expose only `/api/v1/callbacks/`"}}; Alertmanagers and Heartbeats use the same listener, so publish their paths too when they come from outside. Use one Slack app for each Connection ([One Slack app per Connection]({{page "messengers/slack.md#one-slack-app-per-connection"}})).
2. [Connect Slack]({{page "messengers/slack.md#create-the-app-from-a-manifest"}}): create the app from the manifest for Socket Mode or for HTTP. {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "Muster needs these six bot scopes and no other."}} {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "Keep the tab on"}}: the Messages tab carries the codes of Account links ([What the manifest sets]({{page "messengers/slack.md#what-the-manifest-sets"}})).
3. [Connect Slack]({{page "messengers/slack.md#install-the-app-and-create-the-tokens"}}): install the app, copy the bot token and, in Socket Mode, generate {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "a token with the scope `connections:write`"}}, or over HTTP copy the Signing Secret.
4. [Connect Slack]({{page "messengers/slack.md#create-the-connection"}}): create the Connection of type Slack and run **Check connection**. {{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "A bot posts only to channels it is a member of."}} Invite it with `{{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "/invite @Muster"}}` and create a Destination for each channel ([Invite the bot and create Destinations]({{page "messengers/slack.md#invite-the-bot-and-create-destinations"}})).
5. [Link Slack]({{page "messengers/account-links.md#link-slack"}}): people link their accounts with a code that the bot sends by direct message.
6. Finished when a person presses a button of the test message and Muster answers "{{quote "docs/messengers/slack.md" "This is a test message; nothing was changed"}}" ([The test message]({{page "messengers/slack.md#the-test-message"}})).

### Automate through the API

1. [API]({{page "reference/api.md"}}): the conventions of the API and its tokens. {{quote "docs/reference/api.md" "Scripts send a Personal access token (`mstr_pat_`) or a Service account token (`mstr_sat_`) as a bearer token."}} [Sign-in]({{page "sign-in/index.md#ways-to-sign-in"}}) describes both.
2. [`api/openapi.yaml`]({{spec "api/openapi.yaml"}}), {{quote "docs/reference/api.md" "the OpenAPI 3.1 document that the binary serves at `/api/v1/openapi.yaml`"}}.
3. [API tokens and automation]({{page "configure/api-tokens.md"}}): Personal access tokens, Service accounts, the rate limit, `ETag` and `If-Match`, pagination.
4. [Statuses and commands]({{page "concepts/statuses-and-commands.md"}}): the statuses of an Alert Group and the commands that change them.
5. [The command line]({{page "operations/cli.md"}}): {{quote "docs/operations/cli.md" "The commands are for emergencies, for setup and for diagnosis; everything else is in the web UI and the API."}}

